//! Copy-on-write collections: one flat runtime allocation behind an rc
//! box, mutated in place when uniquely owned and cloned once when shared.
//!
//! - `hashmap`, `hashset` — hash-based map and set on the runtime's
//!   hashbrown table, with Reussir-side hashing.
//! - `btreemap`, `btreeset` — ordered map and set on Rust's B-tree,
//!   comparing through the `Ord` bridge.
//! - `vec` — a growable array; `vecdeque` — a double-ended queue over a
//!   ring buffer.
//! - `binaryheap` — a max-priority queue (Rust's `BinaryHeap`; reverse
//!   the `Ord` for a min-heap).
//!
//! Reach for these when usage is linear — build, query, discard — where a
//! flat table beats the `pure` tries on locality and per-operation cost.
//! Reach for `std::collections::pure` when many retained versions branch
//! from a common ancestor: there, every cow update pays a full-table
//! clone, while the persistent structures share unchanged nodes.
//!
//! Element types must be able to cross the FFI boundary: scalars and
//! shared records qualify; `[value]` records, `str`, arrays, and closures
//! do not yet.

pub mod binaryheap;
pub mod btreemap;
pub mod btreeset;
pub mod hashmap;
pub mod hashset;
pub mod vec;
pub mod vecdeque;
